      "correspondence": "An adjusted lift h acts by g and by a generator τ on the ℓth roots. A norm class of divergent reciprocal sum still contains absolute degree-one primes after summably many higher-degree contributions and finitely many exclusions are removed. Descent preserves absolute norm and the Frobenius congruence. Over the fixed field the exponent j of h satisfies j≡1 mod ℓ−1, giving g on L.",
      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "review": "Independently compared the auxiliary fields, finite exclusions, prime norm descent and Frobenius power comparison with the selected proof."
    },
    {
      "id": "DOP-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Arithmetic reciprocity gives the inverse-class prime used for the Jacobi element",
      "anchor": "inputs-class-prime",
      "statement": "For the Hilbert class field of a number field K, using Art(c) as the target gives a prime P with [P]=c. In the cyclotomic application, taking c=[𝔞]⁻¹ and excluding precisely the primes of K dividing the level N gives a principal product 𝔞P and a prime residue field F_p in which the Nth roots remain distinct.",
      "hypotheses": "The input ideal 𝔞 is a nonzero integral ideal of the cyclotomic integer ring. N≥1, the cyclotomic level. The class field Artin map is normalized by arithmetic Frobenius, x↦x^{NP} on residue fields. The chosen P can meet any finite additional exclusions via DOP-01. XEx itself specifies the primes over N.",
      "definitions": [
        "Hilbert class field Artin isomorphism",
        "ideal class of a prime",
        "inertia subgroup",
        "principal product with the chosen prime"
      ],
